===Verb===
{{en-verb|past2=wung|past2_qual=colloquial}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[injure]] slightly (as with a [[gunshot]]), especially in the wing or arm.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[fly]].
#* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|chapter=Afterglow|page=168|passage=Breezes blowing from beds of iris quickened her breath with their perfume; she saw the tufted lilacs sway in the wind, and the streamers of mauve-tinted wistaria swinging, all a-glisten with golden bees; she saw a crimson cardinal '''winging''' through the foliage, and amorous tanagers flashing like scarlet flames athwart the pines.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|of a building}} To add a wing (extra part) to.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To act or speak [[extemporaneous]]ly; to [[improvise]]; to [[wing it]].
#: {{ux|en|I lost all my notes I'd made, so was partially '''winging''' the meeting.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[throw]].
#: {{syn|en|fling|hurl|Thesaurus:throw}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To furnish with wings.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To transport with, or as if with, wings; to bear in flight, or speedily.
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XL|62|⁠Deep folly! yet that this could be—<br>⁠That I could '''wing''' my will with might<br>⁠To leap the grades of life and light,<br>And flash at once, my friend, to thee: {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To traverse by flying.

